<Doc title="parosd" kicker="parosd · rewrite in progress" section="parosd/" siblings={[]}
  description="The journal service: one binary on N machines that serves journals to many tenants and heals itself. It is being rebuilt on the journal v1.">
  <div class="callout status" data-tag="being rebuilt">
    <p>parosd is being rebuilt from the ground up, on top of the <a href={url("journal/")}>journal
    v1</a>. Its design will be written again after the journal. Until then, this page is the only
    parosd page.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what">What it will be</h2>
  <p>One binary, <code>parosd</code>, on N machines of a cell. It serves journals to many tenants
  over the four calls of the journal: <code>Write</code>, <code>Read</code>, <code>Truncate</code>
  and <code>SetLeader</code>. When a disk or a machine is lost, the cell heals through
  reconfiguration, with no operator call. Cells sit in a universe, and a tenant lives in one
  cell.</p>
  <p>paros eats its own food: the state of the service lives in journals, who acts is decided by <code>SetLeader</code> on a journal, and agreement comes from the Paxos flavors that <code>paros-consensus</code> builds. There is no external coordination service to run. The target
  design is <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/docs/architecture`}><code>docs/architecture/</code></a>;
  most of what it names is at the tag below, not in the tree.</p>

  <h2 id="goals">The goals</h2>
  <p>Eleven goals say what paros is for and how it is built. Each rule of the design is a
  consequence of one of them (<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/architecture/01-goal.md`}><code>docs/architecture/01-goal.md</code></a>, §1.1).</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>A journal service for tenants.</strong> Four calls, and a durable, ordered, fenced log. paros owns the log, never the state.</li>
    <li><strong>The cell heals with nobody watching.</strong> After the faults stop, each journal is whole again within one bound, or reports a true reason.</li>
    <li><strong>paros eats its own food.</strong> State is in journals, who acts is an election on a journal, and agreement is a Paxos flavor of the core.</li>
    <li><strong>One shape at every level.</strong> The universe, a cell and a tenant are each a control journal, a pure fold and one coordinator.</li>
    <li><strong>The person says what, the system says where.</strong> A person types names and what a journal must survive. The system chooses the machines and the ids.</li>
    <li><strong>Everything can move.</strong> A namespace moves to another cell with no change of protocol.</li>
    <li><strong>Layers that stand alone.</strong> Each layer is a crate that another program can use alone. The core has no tenant.</li>
    <li><strong>Every role is a pure actor.</strong> A deterministic step, persist before send, and I/O only in adapters.</li>
    <li><strong>Proved before built.</strong> No code before its TLA+ model, and no excuse in simulation.</li>
    <li><strong>Everything simulates, and it runs in the browser.</strong> Every layer and the whole service run in the deterministic simulation, and the same code compiles to wasm.</li>
    <li><strong>Easy to operate.</strong> The service heals itself and scales itself, so a person runs it with few actions.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The features of the first version stay. Its structure changes: the service becomes a stack
  of layers on the journal v1, and each layer is a crate with its own model and its own
  simulation (<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/architecture/04-roles.md`}><code>docs/architecture/04-roles.md</code></a>, §4.4).</p>

  <h2 id="before">The version before the rewrite</h2>
  <p>A first version ran: a daemon on each machine, a CLI, many cells, Biscuit tokens and a
  deterministic simulation with faults on. It showed where its foundations were too weak, so paros
  started again from the consensus core. The whole first version stays at the git tag <a href={TAG}><code>v0.2.0-alpha.1</code></a>: <code>parosd</code>, <code>parosctl</code>, <code>paros-bench</code>, the Docker Compose demo and the simulation. To run it:</p>
  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/PierreZ/paros && cd paros
git checkout v0.2.0-alpha.1
docker compose up -d --build
docker compose run --rm init
docker compose run --rm parosctl tenant create acme</code></pre>
  <p>Its <code>DEMO.md</code> is the walk-through. A recap of each removed crate is in <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/docs/archive/v0.2.0-alpha.1`}><code>docs/archive/v0.2.0-alpha.1/</code></a>.</p>
